#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163101} :activity/id #uuid "5a4d4eca-048a-559f-8986-fcbff586afd0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050971 :topic/id #uuid "767f9ccb-0c08-5d76-97c4-5882b4f99cd1" :topic/math-academy-id 3004} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163101 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163341 :tutorial/content "The **joint cumulative distribution function** (or joint CDF) of two discrete random variables $X,$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P( X \\leq x, Y \\leq y)  = \\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i).\n$$\n\nThe sets $S_X$ and $S_Y,$ on which the variables $X$ and $Y$ are defined, are the **supports** of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWe can see from this definition that the joint CDF is analogous to the cumulative distribution function of a single random variable.\n\nConsider the joint PMF for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nHere, the supports of $X$ and $Y$ are $S_X = \\{ 0, 1 \\}$ and $S_Y = \\{ 0, 1, 2 \\},$ respectively. \n\nAs an example, let's compute $F(1,1).$ By definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,1) & =P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X \\leq 1, Y \\leq 1.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,1) & =f(0,0)+f(0,1)+f(1,0)+f(1,1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.2+0.3+0.2 \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "68282cd4-eb49-54e1-a97a-8d49988c8922" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6430 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "494e3329-5465-5724-b614-4ecacfaaed97" :step/math-academy-id 38838 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163102}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163102 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162124 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161690 :question/id #uuid "a4b51a4b-0571-55e9-a3ea-881ffce4839b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10303"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3c48f82-c3ed-5014-b1f5-cd9e8de542f0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051001}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481415}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Joint CDF"} :step/id #uuid "f666bcd7-dca8-55b3-aedf-a8ef91edbe96" :step/math-academy-id 38839 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163152}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163103 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162125 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161691 :question/id #uuid "115c9ea4-0415-5249-8a4f-a7113e022681" :question/math-academy-id "e-10304"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02f8da68-ea1e-5f20-8f1a-7a38508e1f1f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481437}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Cumulative Probabilities Given Values From the Joint CDF"} :step/id #uuid "59dbcbcc-3e03-5e5e-946c-753994f1060e" :step/math-academy-id 38840 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163109}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163108 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162130 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161696 :question/id #uuid "28277187-2842-5b7a-bfee-672bd4f08aca" :question/math-academy-id "e-10336"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b8dd2d-ecaa-58bf-baa3-72dc8f37523f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481416}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Joint CDF Geometrically"} :step/id #uuid "5ccba458-0f60-53d1-9e72-3de4cf2a6b1f" :step/math-academy-id 38938 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163103}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163109 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162131 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161697 :question/id #uuid "0b991ee4-a4b3-55b4-93d1-77936f5b3067" :question/math-academy-id "e-10337"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b97d9a7a-fa12-5d8d-8958-39625f117979" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481438}] :knowledge-point/title "Further Finding Cumulative Probabilities Given Values From the Joint CDF"} :step/id #uuid "2851f1ef-e083-54cd-a8c0-7dd89a1c3344" :step/math-academy-id 38939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163360 :tutorial/content "Consider the joint PMF for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nWe can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\neq 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n![](images/c6/c6d8e4012a0a16f865bf229549512ff772e103d3fcd7667738cf7250d7267e84.png)\n\n \n\nNow, the joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = \\sum\\limits_{x_i \\leq x} \\sum\\limits_{y_i \\leq y} f(x_i, y_i).\n$$\n\nGeometrically, then, the joint CDF gives the total \"probability mass\" that lies inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nFor example, consider the point $(2,1.5)$ and the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at this point, shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46837da3e8f8f44831da4e6b464e9f8562f322b17f584df3fc87912d1c09d4c4.png)\n\nThe value of $F(2,1.5)$ equals the total \"probability mass\" contained within this rectangle. That is,\n\n$$\nF(2,1.5) = f(1,0) + f(2,0) + f(1,1) + f(2,1).\n$$\n\nIt's worth highlighting a few important cases:\n\n- If our rectangular region doesn't include any points where the probability is nonzero, then we must have $F(x,y) = 0.$\n\n![](images/bb/bb5faa2b42c9ece2fe80cff4dd27a8b720cb363f5319c3cb5f5dd065f818c403.png)\n\n- If our rectangular region includes *all* points where the probability is nonzero, then we must have $F(x,y) = 1.$\n\n![](images/81/811b15e3cea46e0a4a546e68217a2994a7b2a7b80d6bc444bc6fbaa2ebaed355.png)\n\nFinally, we note the following properties of the joint CDF:\n\n- $0 \\leq F(x,y) \\leq 1$\n\n- The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by $F_X(x) = F(x, \\infty).$\n\n- The marginal CDF of $Y$ is given by $F_Y(y) = F( \\infty, y).$\n\n- $F(\\infty, \\infty) = 1$\n\n- $F(-\\infty,y) = F(x, -\\infty) = 0$\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables, then $F(x,y) = F_X(x) \\cdot F_Y(y).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d6a285ac-6fdf-506c-9228-20857fedc5b3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6977 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Joint CDF"} :step/id #uuid "5d0f7f20-6219-5e6b-9d38-ad689f056506" :step/math-academy-id 42192 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163108}}] :activity/title "The Joint CDF of Two Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162165}]}
